  "thought": "I will apply the routing policy to each request carefully.\n\nI've mapped the four models to their effort ranges and lane families. Atlas-pro covers implement, review, and research at high to max effort. Atlas-lite handles extract and prose at low to high. Corvid-9 covers implement and review at medium to max. Corvid-mini handles extract at low to medium.\n\nI've added the Pellet model to the routing table. It handles prose and research at medium to high effort.\n\nThe lane priority order is now set. Implement goes to atlas-pro first, then corvid-9. Review goes to corvid-9 first, then atlas-pro. Research goes to atlas-pro first, then pellet-2. Extract goes to corvid-mini first, then atlas-lite. Prose goes to pellet-2 first, then atlas-lite.\n\nI also noted the eligibility rules. A model must have the lane in its lanes column. For review requests, the reviewer family must differ from the author's family. If a request pins a CLI, only models on that CLI are eligible. Any model the request lists as unavailable is skipped.\n\nI've added the final two rules. If no model is eligible, the answer is \"Unrouted\" with null effort. Effort values are clamped to a model's floor or ceiling when needed, but they never block eligibility.\n\nI started applying the policy to request q1. Atlas-pro is eligible for implement lane and receives effort high.\n\nRequest q2 routes to atlas-pro for review. Corvid-9 is ineligible because its family matches the author's family. Effort stays high.\n\nRequest q3 pins CLI pl. No model on that CLI handles extract, so this request has no eligible model.",
